<title>MADAGASCAR: Madagascar says EU ban will cost it $80 million.</title>
<headline>Madagascar says EU ban will cost it $80 million.</headline>
<dateline>ANTANANARIVO 1997-08-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>Officials in Madagascar said on Tuesday the island would lose some $80 million a year if the European Union went ahead with a ban of its exports of meat and seafood.</p>
<p>The European Commission in a statement on Tuesday said the proposed cut-off date of August 15 on grounds of hygiene would be re-examined by a meeting of veterinary experts on Thursday.</p>
<p>Malagasy officials say a ban would be a hard blow for one of the world's poorest countries. Meat and seafood exports are Madagascar's second largest hard currency earners after coffee.</p>
<p>An EU source said salmonella bacteria were found in some shipments from Madagascar. An official said the government was demanding a second analysis of the allegedly contaminated food.</p>
</text>
